10-605 Mini-project: starter code

See the project document for the rules, deadlines and grading. This file only covers how to run the
starter code.


SETUP (on an AWS g5.xlarge, one A10G GPU)

    pip install torch numpy huggingface_hub
    git clone https://github.com/aditya-gupta-04/10605-f26-btm-miniproj.git
    cd 10605-f26-btm-miniproj
    python3 code/download_data.py

download_data.py fetches the six models (models/) and the training pool (pool/) from Hugging Face,
about 2 GB, and checks every file against MANIFEST.sha256. Run long jobs inside tmux so they survive
an SSH disconnect.


WHAT IS HERE

    models/seed.bin              the shared starting point: GPT-2 small, 124M parameters
    models/generalist.bin        the seed, trained further on the general mix
    models/expert_<domain>.bin   the seed, trained further on one domain (math, code, medicine, history)
    pool/<domain>.bin            training data: 60M tokens per domain
    dev/<domain>.bin             dev set: about 1M tokens per domain
    code/gpt.py                  the models' architecture: load, run and save the provided checkpoints
    code/data.py                 read the token files (GPT-2 token ids)
    code/create_submission.py    check a solution, score it on the dev set, write submission.log
    code/download_data.py        fetch models/ and pool/
    solutions/                   two placeholder solutions to copy and change


RUN THE PLACEHOLDERS

    python3 solutions/average_experts.py --out runs/average
        No training: the four experts' weights, averaged. Takes under a minute.

    python3 solutions/short_training.py --out runs/short
        The generalist plus one extra layer, trained for 200 steps on an equal mix of the four pools.
        Takes about 6 minutes. Options: --steps, --batch-size, --micro-batch, --lr, --warmup
        (see --help).

    python3 code/create_submission.py runs/short
        Checks the model, scores it on the dev set and writes runs/short/submission.log.

Each placeholder writes an output directory with its weights, model_def.py and train.log.


YOUR OWN SOLUTION

Your training script writes an output directory containing:

    model_def.py   defines load_model(directory, device), which returns a torch.nn.Module that maps
                   token ids [batch, length] (int64) to logits [batch, length, 50257]
    your weights   in any format
    train.log      your training log, in any format

Any architecture is allowed. Put its class in model_def.py, or in files next to it, so that
load_model can rebuild the model. The two placeholders show both cases:

    solutions/gpt_model_def.py           the provided architecture: load_model is one line
    solutions/extra_layer_model_def.py   a changed architecture: the class, plus load_model

A good way to start is to copy solutions/short_training.py and change what you need. It is split into
labeled sections: settings, model and weight loading, data, optimizer, train loop, saving.

Two things to keep from the starter code when you work with the provided models:
  - save in the provided format with gpt.save or gpt.write_checkpoint, which keep the models' muP
    settings (a checkpoint saved without them scores about 9);
  - train with gpt.optimizer_groups, which sets the per-group learning rates these models need.


SUBMITTING

    python3 code/create_submission.py <output directory>

Then upload <output directory>/submission.log, your train.log and your code to Gradescope, as the
project document describes.
